# Repo-root .gitignore for the Paladin monorepo. Path-specific ignores live in
# backend/ and frontend/ where applicable; this file covers the cross-cutting
# cases.
#
# Every rule here is load-bearing — it matches something this tree actually
# produces. What was removed when the repository was prepared for publication,
# and why:
#
#   *.exe, *.exe~, *.dll, *.so, *.dylib   no Windows and no plugin builds; the
#                                         backend image sets CGO_ENABLED=0 and
#                                         nothing in the tree imports "C"
#   *.test                                nothing runs `go test -c`
#   coverage.*, *.coverprofile, profile.cov
#                                         the task library writes
#                                         coverage/cover.out, which none of the
#                                         three matched — `coverage/` below does
#   Thumbs.db                             Windows only
#   backend/tests/{grpcurl,api/mutations,api/security}/reports/
#                                         no such directory, and nothing writes
#                                         one
#   backend/cmd/seed-fixture/main.go.bak  one editor's scratch file from one
#                                         afternoon
#   docs/BACKLOG.md                       BACKLOG.md is at the root here
#   commented-out .idea/ and .vscode/     a rule that does nothing
#
# A rule that matches nothing is worse than absent: it reads as protection, so
# the next person does not look for the thing it was supposed to cover.

# ── Compiled binaries ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# `go build ./...` in backend/ names each binary after its main package and
# leaves it in the working directory. Both of backend/cmd/'s packages land
# here. `!server/` because the name also belongs to a directory in some trees
# and a directory is not the artefact.
server
!server/
backend/seed-fixture

# ── Code generators ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The codegen task installs the protoc plugins a module pins into its ./bin
# before running buf.
sdk/go/bin/

# ── Coverage ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The shared task library runs `go test -coverprofile=coverage/cover.out`, so
# the directory is the artefact rather than any one file name.
coverage/
*.out

# ── Go workspace files ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Three modules live here — backend/, capability/ and the root's own tooling —
# which is exactly the shape that invites a `go work init` to bridge them. A
# committed go.work silently overrides every module's own dependency
# resolution, so CI would stop building what a release builds.
go.work
go.work.sum

# ── Dotenv ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Secrets never belong in the tree. backend/deploy/.env is the one exception
# and is negated explicitly rather than left to contradict the rule above it:
# it carries DOCKER_BUILDKIT and COMPOSE_DOCKER_CLI_BUILD, which compose reads
# and which every developer needs identically.
.env
.env.*
!**/.env.example
!backend/deploy/.env

# ── OS cruft ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
.DS_Store

# ── Generated build metadata ────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Written by the Taskfile's metadata:write task on every `task deploy`.
# Versions and timestamps reach git through tags and commit history, not
# through a tracked file that changes on every build.
**/deploy/info.env

# ── Hypothesis ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Runtime cache for the Python property-based tests.
.hypothesis/

# ── Task ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Task's fingerprint cache and remote-Taskfile cache.
**/.task/*
# ...except the remote lock. Task refuses to run when the content behind a
# ?ref= no longer matches it, which is the only thing standing between a moved
# tag and this build. Kept at any depth, because a component run from its own
# directory caches there rather than at the root.
!**/.task/remote/
**/.task/remote/*
!**/.task/remote/*.checksum
# ...and the module bodies themselves. Committing them is what lets
# `task --offline` resolve every include from the repository: no clone, so no
# race over the one shared clone directory, no download timeout, and a build
# that does not depend on github.com being reachable. The .timestamp files stay
# out — they are per-machine cache state, not content.
!**/.task/remote/*.yaml

# ── Local-only lefthook overrides ──────────────────────────────────────────
# Gates that belong to a working copy rather than to the project; lefthook
# merges the file automatically when it is present.
lefthook-local.yml

# ── Local development tooling ───────────────────────────────────────────────
# Assistant instructions, agent rules and skills, the spec workflow's scripts
# and the token-filter config stay on the machine that uses them.
.claude/
.agents/
.specify/
.rtk/
**/skills-lock.json

# ── Release tooling (.github/release: semantic-release only) ────────────────
/.github/release/node_modules/
# Where it lived before; a checkout from then still has one.
/node_modules/

**/CLAUDE.md
